# Day 12/50 — Database Normalization

Imagine having the same information written in five different places.

Every time something changes, you'd have to update it everywhere. Miss one place, and suddenly things don't match.

That's exactly the kind of problem database normalization tries to solve.
It organizes data into separate, related tables, reduces unnecessary repetition, and helps maintain consistency.

And honestly, there's something interesting about that.

We often think having more information means having a better understanding. But sometimes, repeating the same things everywhere only creates confusion.
Not everything needs to be duplicated to remain important.

**Sometimes, keeping things simple is what keeps them consistent.**
